{"h":3395,"lines":[{"w":0.058207766215006507,"h":0.014123895283441823,"y":0.099373859593836489,"t":"Test 2","c":1,"x":0.12730017499752319},{"t":"READING PASSAGE 3","h":0.017428299282689519,"w":0.29044692993164067,"y":0.14206226764511598,"c":1,"x":0.1274238979676853},{"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 27-40, which are based on Reading","y":0.17726737340418142,"c":1,"w":0.77572016398111976,"h":0.017500068610632802,"x":0.12757201666973647},{"t":"Passage 3 below.","w":0.15843621571858724,"c":1,"x":0.12757201545191066,"y":0.19464075410030324,"h":0.016195524146054074},{"w":0.18106995900472006,"t":"Having a laugh","x":0.43004115208324173,"y":0.22965116259557794,"h":0.020348837575025147,"c":1},{"x":0.17695472047230454,"h":0.018944996405827252,"t":"The findings of psychological scientists reveal the importance of humour","y":0.26884570087340709,"c":1,"w":0.68930043538411456},{"x":0.12750589871667009,"h":0.018344301217015246,"t":"Humans start developing a sense of humour as early as six weeks old, when babies begin to laugh","c":1,"w":0.78202580769856778,"y":0.30193207947781264},{"h":0.019345143096887951,"t":"and smile in response to stimuli. Laughter is universal across all human cultures and even exists","w":0.76976623535156252,"c":1,"x":0.12745338842769741,"y":0.31780777876107724},{"t":"in some form in rats, chimps, and bonobos. Like other human emotions and expressions, laughter","x":0.12551439345268364,"w":0.78600824991861973,"h":0.017508628652008751,"c":1,"y":0.33568904592095805},{"t":"and humour provide psychological scientists with rich resources for studying human psychology,","x":0.12757202489553662,"w":0.77777776082356764,"y":0.35306242662848308,"c":1,"h":0.016195524146054185},{"y":0.36918604633550978,"h":0.017445284570484998,"t":"ranging from the development of language to the neuroscience of social perception.","w":0.6748971048990885,"c":1,"x":0.12551441099352853},{"c":1,"h":0.017316074056816344,"y":0.40223405131453327,"t":"Theories focusing on the evolution of laughter point to it as an important adaptation for social","x":0.12751510715839326,"w":0.75321716308593745},{"x":0.12749998524322109,"y":0.41696959131378097,"c":1,"w":0.75732508341471361,"t":"communication. Take, for example, the recorded laughter in TV comedy shows. Back in 1950,","h":0.020981967238571153},{"w":0.72633743286132812,"h":0.01598837238037687,"c":1,"y":0.43604651147817397,"t":"US sound engineer Charley Douglass hated dealing with the unpredictable laughter of live","x":0.12757202436073764},{"w":0.74507237752278643,"y":0.4506767148527846,"c":1,"t":"audiences, so started recording his own 'laugh tracks'. These were intended to help people at","x":0.12746451070292267,"h":0.020322575024356526},{"w":0.77777776082356764,"t":"home feel like they were in a social situation, such as a crowded theatre. Douglass even recorded","x":0.12551441170217392,"y":0.4693757360005848,"h":0.016195524146054296,"c":1},{"h":0.018753786390324767,"c":1,"x":0.1275236710290317,"y":0.48542655339698726,"t":"various types of laughter, as well as mixtures of laughter from men, women, and children. In","w":0.74084869384765617},{"w":0.78629277547200527,"h":0.021559834339874184,"c":1,"t":"doing so, he picked up on a quality of laughter that is now interesting researchers: a simple 'haha'","x":0.12745557736606863,"y":0.49995155051118578},{"w":0.55555557250976562,"h":0.016019188192342826,"c":1,"x":0.12757200788968523,"t":"communicates a remarkable amount of socially relevant information.","y":0.52031802125007465},{"c":1,"t":"In one study conducted in 2016, samples of laughter from pairs of English-speaking students","x":0.12749250392410943,"y":0.55123731299669609,"w":0.7450236002604167,"h":0.019414739979450757},{"c":1,"w":0.7345679219563801,"x":0.12757200603310717,"h":0.01598837238037687,"y":0.56976744171374871,"t":"were recorded at the University of California, Santa Cruz. A team made up of more than 30"},{"t":"psychological scientists, anthropologists, and biologists then played these recordings to listeners","h":0.018584522117012559,"c":1,"y":0.58542006345970587,"x":0.12750149389945317,"w":0.77173416137695316},{"c":1,"y":0.60207042979562697,"t":"from 24 diverse societies, from indigenous tribes in New Guinea to city-dwellers in India and","x":0.12750178959559566,"h":0.018136475476555947,"w":0.75117853800455725},{"x":0.12752573654112212,"h":0.01791379701684026,"t":"Europe. Participants were asked whether they thought the people laughing were friends or","w":0.72232849121093756,"y":0.61900848197352243,"c":1},{"w":0.77160491943359388,"c":1,"y":0.63662790681349779,"h":0.017441860778592999,"t":"strangers. On average, the results were remarkably consistent: worldwide, people's guesses were","x":0.12757202587453653},{"x":0.12742093500512786,"y":0.65282981362197579,"w":0.31918754577636715,"h":0.017716747571498104,"c":1,"t":"correct approximately 60% of the time."},{"h":0.01598837238037687,"y":0.68604651147890072,"x":0.12757201239305374,"c":1,"t":"Researchers have also found that different types of laughter serve as codes to complex human","w":0.74897120157877617},{"t":"social hierarchies. A team led by Christopher Oveis from the University of California, San","x":0.12748267893204107,"w":0.72649490356445323,"h":0.019437554025818482,"y":0.70092232420225375,"c":1},{"c":1,"y":0.76874164585382698,"h":0.018303619537533233,"x":0.12751990219809956,"t":"assigned to groups of four, with each group composed of two low-status members, who had just","w":0.77171819051106771},{"h":0.016195524146054185,"w":0.77366256713867188,"x":0.12551439553891536,"y":0.78621908103020255,"t":"joined their college fraternity group, and two high-status members, older students who had been","c":1},{"y":0.80377906962126688,"x":0.12757201855547115,"h":0.016008916255020877,"t":"active in the fraternity for at least two years. Laughter was recorded as each student took a turn","w":0.76337448120117191,"c":1},{"t":"at being teased by the others, involving the use of mildly insulting nicknames. Analysis revealed","w":0.77181640625000003,"h":0.019856058666647081,"c":1,"x":0.12748119182860099,"y":0.81827362436856022},{"y":0.83560748004025098,"w":0.76760904947916664,"t":"that, as expected, high-status individuals produced more dominant laughs and fewer submissive","h":0.018604421222448653,"x":0.12751496426034425,"c":1},{"h":0.017667844522968212,"x":0.12757200856485165,"y":0.85306242661540888,"w":0.77366256713867176,"t":"laughs relative to the low-status individuals. Meanwhile, low-status individuals were more likely","c":1},{"c":1,"t":"to change their laughter based on their position of power; that is, the newcomers produced more","w":0.76968892415364587,"x":0.12750872574654135,"y":0.86891865524973022,"h":0.019180088919380167},{"y":0.91715116261368301,"x":0.12962962972343695,"t":"44","w":0.024691357612609849,"h":0.013081395583944722,"c":1}],"w":2400}